## GBA Technical Notes

Hollow Shore is designed to run within GBA hardware constraints. The following decisions reflect those constraints directly.

**GBA Hardware Specs:**

| Spec | Value |
|---|---|
| CPU | 32-bit ARM7TDMI @ 16.78 MHz |
| RAM | 256KB internal + 32KB video RAM |
| VRAM | 96KB total |
| Screen | 240×160 pixels, 15-bit color |
| Max Sprites | 128 on screen simultaneously |
| Cart ROM | Up to 32MB |
| Battery Save | 32KB–128KB SRAM via cartridge |

**Feasibility Summary:**

| System | Approach | Feasibility |
|---|---|---|
| Grid movement + top-down view | Native GBA tile engine — ideal fit | ✓ Perfect |
| Tile-based building | Standard tile placement on background layer | ✓ Perfect |
| Day/night cycle | Palette swapping — near-zero CPU cost | ✓ Easy |
| Farming + crafting + inventory | Menu-driven logic, lightweight | ✓ Achievable |
| Combat + enemy AI | Capped at 8–10 enemies on screen simultaneously | ✓ Achievable |
| Weather effects | Palette shifts + single tiling overlay sprite | ✓ Achievable |
| Dungeon fog of war | GBA hardware window feature — built-in masking | ✓ Hardware native |
| Boss fights | One complex sprite, 2–3 secondary sprites | ✓ Manageable |
| 9-section chunk loading | One section in VRAM at a time, fade-to-black transition | ✓ Clean solution |
| 3 preset world maps | Replaces procedural generation — fits in ROM | ✓ Solves RAM limit |
| Save system | 128KB SRAM + compressed bitfield encoding | ⚠ Needs 128KB cart |
| Siege events | Capped wave sizes, staged spawns | ⚠ Enemy count managed |